item.text:
{
  "instrument": {
    "state": "Act on the next fortnight of deliveries, their handling histories and temperature measurements. Retain competing explanations: warming during staging or loading, warming in transit, warming while awaiting receipt, differences or errors in receiving measurement, and combinations of these. Acceptable loading-room records do not establish individual package temperatures at departure, and one receiving reading does not locate when warming occurred. Assume staff can identify a small sample of packages and record additional handling times; confirm logger suitability and permitted placement before using them.",
    "operation": "Before sampling, write down what evidence would support or weaken each explanation. Rotate the limited loggers through selected deliveries spanning all three bays, both carriers, shorter and longer routes, and different receiving methods; include deliveries without reported exceptions. Do not claim complete coverage or independently separable comparisons. Identify sampled packages and link each to its bay, carrier, route and receiving site. Check logger and receiving-device agreement under the same stable conditions, synchronize clocks, and choose a logging interval short enough to distinguish relevant handling stages within device capacity. Follow the same sampled package from refrigerated staging through loading, transit and receipt, recording staging exit, loading completion, departure, arrival and receiving-measurement times. Record logger placement, measurement method, relevant delays and accessible handling observations. Where feasible, pair the site's usual receiving reading with a promptly taken standardized reading on the same package using a suitable checked device and an approved method; record any difference in timing or measurement location. Review traces and paired readings as they become available, comparing similar deliveries and revising the competing explanations. During the investigation, reinforce required refrigeration, minimize avoidable unrefrigerated staging and receiving delays, and standardize approved receiving measurements. Record when these changes begin. Follow existing food-handling and disposition requirements immediately for any suspected breach; do not wait for the investigation. Add more targeted temporary changes only when observations support the relevant stage, and track their effect without deliberately exposing food to unsafe conditions.",
    "signal": "Look for proposed discriminating patterns: a rise before departure supports staging/loading exposure; a rise after departure and before arrival supports transit exposure; a rise between arrival and the receiving reading supports receiving delay. These patterns require reliable stage times and a logger placement that meaningfully represents the sampled package's thermal conditions. Repeated disagreement between usual and standardized readings, especially when associated with method or device, supports measurement differences. Agreement between readings weakens that explanation for those observations. Multiple rises or a rise combined with measurement disagreement support multiple contributors. Bay room-temperature records, route duration alone, missing stage boundaries, or a logger measuring air while staff measure product may fail to distinguish explanations. Compare both exception and non-exception deliveries and actively seek observations inconsistent with the current leading explanation.",
    "inference": "Use the emerging patterns to prioritize the next investigation and proportionate handling changes, rather than declare a single cause. Repeated predeparture warming would justify concentrating observations on staging dwell and loading practices; transit warming would justify checking the implicated carrier's refrigeration operation and handling events; receiving-stage warming would justify prompt refrigerated receipt; reproducible measurement disagreement would justify device checks and method harmonization. Treat these as conditional follow-ups, not established findings. Lower an explanation's priority only when the observations could reasonably have detected its predicted pattern. At the fortnight's end, summarize supported, weakened and unresolved explanations, sampling gaps and interim actions. End the added two-week observation effort unless further work is authorized; if evidence remains inconclusive, propose a focused follow-up aimed at the unresolved distinction and retain required protective handling.",
    "limit": "This procedure can narrow plausible contributors but cannot guarantee a unique cause or demonstrate that every delivery meets requirements. A small, nonrandom sample may miss intermittent events and cannot establish their prevalence. Multiple readings from one package or trip are correlated, and bay, carrier, route and customer differences may be confounded. Logger placement, response lag, calibration uncertainty, clock error and single-package sampling limit detection and attribution; environmental temperature is not automatically product temperature. Paired methods may disagree because they measure different locations or at different times. Interim handling changes also alter the conditions being investigated, so improved outcomes alone do not prove causation. Retain alternatives absent a sensitive, relevant test, consider how frequently each condition occurs among sampled deliveries, and reopen the hypothesis set when observations fit none of the candidates. Stop or modify any added measurement procedure that compromises food handling or routine operations."
  }
}
